7] [17592186129337 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129337 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "089b1802-0940-53e5-af98-b7a9c26bba1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129421 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=5$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e2508fb-892a-523d-953d-53a729ceb055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129421 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}5\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =5({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =5(4-2)(5-1) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=40\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 40=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=40.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129423 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x-y+2.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2fe7b45-b659-56e0-83bf-d42ccd4288ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129423 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $L(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x-y+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x-y+2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}=f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j})={x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2\n$$\n\nand the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129854 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "186fcf52-8571-5f00-9ea4-41ddd0233cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129854 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1\\le 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+1\\le 2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1\\le ({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+1\\le 2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+1]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{j-1}+{x}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({5}^{2}-{2}^{2})(2-(-2))=84 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({2}^{2}-(-2)^{2})(5-2)=0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =(5-2)(2-(-2))=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 0-84+12\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le -72\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-72.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1061 22783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02381e3e-cb34-5f3b-8940-d1b55826683c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1061 22946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ef801e2-e7f6-542e-aab3-64391c418f88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1061 35550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5af32a1a-efb5-509d-a196-f411e5d46fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1061 35559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38972544-74f5-555d-aadd-8581d0da7887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130122 #edb/ref 1061 35700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c30b46b-78e4-5a7f-bec5-89ef0019d000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a positive, continuous function defined on a closed and bounded rectangle $R,$ and let $P = P_1\\times P_2$ be a partition of $R.$ We know that the volume $V$ bounded by the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ in the $xy$-plane satisfies\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq V \\leq U(f,P),\n$$\n\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the lower and upper Riemann sums of $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\nImagine that our initial partition $P$ consists of a few rectangles only. Let's visualize the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, as shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89ad0fd33f34cfad4f55fc2085360878460df92479f5f938ecd254420457f3f4.png)\n\n \n\nThen, we refine $P$ by adding more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ resulting in more rectangles in $P{:}$ \n\n![](images/56/5635f58e19c47d6a9cfeab49c298ce5ca7aa1d8697e1b53390bb3bc31859637c.png)\n\n \n\nRefining the partition once again, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b2/b22f267ecf28627404eb90928e44d59b2873e88adc22ffaaa0a1a3335fffa307.png)\n\n \n\nAs we continue to refine $P$ by adding more and more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ we find that the values of the lower and upper Riemann sums approach the volume $V{:}$\n\n- $L(f, P)\\to V$ from below, and\n\n- $U(f, P) \\to V$ from above.\n\nWe define the **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1065 3884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b9ba8fdf-255d-53c2-832a-34ed3374abb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130379 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've defined the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ (if it exists) that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Up to now, we've assumed that $f(x,y)$ is continuous and $R$ is closed and bounded. Whenever this is the case, a unique number $I$ will always exist.\n\n- For positive, continuous functions, the double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$\n\n- More generally, the double integral gives a signed volume. If $f$ is negative everywhere on $R,$ then the double integral of $f$ over $R$ is negative. If $f$ is both positive and negative on $R,$ then the double integral gives the difference between the positive and negative unsigned volumes. This is similar to the case with definite integrals for single-valued functions.\n\n- Finally, It's possible to use this definition with functions that are not continuous. If a function $f$ (not necessarily continuous) is defined over $R,$ we say that $f$ is **Riemann integrable** over $R$ if a unique number $I$ can be found that satisfies the above inequality. Many (though not all) non-continuous functions are Riemann integrable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1065 5824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b0889acb-60da-5e9c-9f1b-530b118b05f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130417 #edb/ref 1067 "Final Remarks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257417 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4863d036-e3c0-54ea-9b68-c3d810c25830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257425 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257425 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257425 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4985370e-205f-57cd-96e5-3dd1860f3c40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258113 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258113 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258113 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "96b6bede-bb0d-536c-9929-13c0f977e7a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258253 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258253 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258253 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a8c42faf-b85f-56d6-b9cb-3abd2bfcc348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258475 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258475 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258475 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1771016-b30c-5b16-ad3d-44d9508f2a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186257425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259483 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffcc488d-5039-58c5-9ed9-42126f86d743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259569 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=1$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bde095b1-d9b2-5272-b46e-003fde137023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259569 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}1\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =(-2-(-5))(7-3) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=12\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 12=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428526 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428526 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1990->1930. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428526 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048942 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428527 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428527 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing cov